    <body>
      <sec id="article-124054.s1" sec-type="Continuing Education Activity">
        <title>Continuing Education Activity</title>
        <p>The postpartum period, often called the fourth trimester, begins immediately after childbirth and generally lasts 6 to 8 weeks, although recovery timelines vary among individuals. During this phase, the maternal body undergoes substantial physiologic changes while gradually returning toward the prepregnancy state. Uterine involution, healing of perineal or surgical tissue, cessation of postpartum bleeding, and stabilization of hormonal levels occur during this time. Recovery also involves adjustment to lactation, sleep disruption, and new caregiving responsibilities. Emotional and psychological changes frequently accompany these physical transitions, and vulnerability to mood disturbances may increase. Comprehensive postpartum care focuses on monitoring maternal recovery, identifying complications, supporting infant bonding, and promoting overall well-being for both mother and newborn.</p>
        <p>This educational activity provides clinicians with practical knowledge to recognize physiologic changes, identify complications, and support recovery during the postpartum period. Course content emphasizes assessment of maternal physical healing, screening for mood disturbances, counseling regarding lactation and infant care, and education on recovery expectations. Clinicians gain strategies for communicating effectively with new mothers and addressing physical, emotional, and psychosocial needs during the early weeks after childbirth. Collaboration within an interprofessional healthcare team&#x02014;including nursing, lactation specialists, mental health professionals, and other maternal health specialists&#x02014;strengthens continuity of care and promotes early identification of complications. Coordinated teamwork supports comprehensive postpartum management and improves maternal and neonatal outcomes.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Objectives:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Determine appropriate follow-up intervals, monitoring strategies, and risk stratification based on maternal comorbidities, delivery complications, and psychosocial factors.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Create individualized postpartum care plans that incorporate maternal recovery needs, breastfeeding or lactation support, sleep considerations, contraception counseling, and newborn care education.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Identify&#x000a0;the common medical conditions that women encounter during the postpartum period.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Communicate&#x000a0;how interprofessional team coordination can enhance patient outcomes when rendering a new mother's postpartum care.</p></list-item></list>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=124054&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=124054">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-124054.s2" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>The postpartum period starts shortly after delivery, typically lasts 6 to 8 weeks, and concludes when the mother's body&#x000a0;has nearly returned to its prepregnant state.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-124054.r1">[1]</xref> The weeks following childbirth are crucial for establishing the long-term health and well-being of both the mother and infant. Implementing a consistent, tailored postpartum care plan is essential, as most maternal and infant deaths occur within the first month. Effective postpartum care is vital to enhance both short-term and long-term health outcomes for the mother and newborn.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-124054.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-124054.r2">[2]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-124054.s3" sec-type="Function">
        <title>Function</title>
        <p>
<bold>Timing of Postnatal Visits</bold>
</p>
        <p>In April 2018, the American College of Obstetrics and Gynecology (ACOG) recommended 12 weeks of support rather than a single 6-week postpartum visit. ACOG also recommends a postpartum evaluation within the first 3 weeks after delivery, either in person or by phone, followed by ongoing care as needed, and concluding with a comprehensive postpartum visit no later than 12 weeks.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-124054.r3">[3]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Common Postpartum Care Issues</bold>
</p>
        <p>
<bold>Vaginal pain</bold>
</p>
        <p>Genital tract trauma is typical&#x000a0;with spontaneous vaginal delivery.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-124054.r4">[4]</xref>&#x000a0;Mild vaginal tears may occur during delivery and take a few weeks to heal, whereas extensive tears may take significantly longer to heal. Patients should be advised&#x000a0;to take over-the-counter medications such as ibuprofen or acetaminophen for pain, sit on a padded ring, and/or cool the area with an ice pack to relieve the pain. Healthcare professionals should inform women about the signs of infection, such as fever, and encourage them to seek medical attention for persistent, severe pain.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-124054.r5">[5]</xref>&#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0;</p>
        <p>
<bold>Vaginal bleeding/discharge</bold>
</p>
        <p>Bloody vaginal discharge (lochia rubra) is heavy for the first 3 to 4 days postpartum, and over time, the discharge becomes watery, with the color changing to pinkish-brown (lochia serosa). The discharge changes to yellowish-white after 10 to 12 days (lochia alba).&#x000a0;Patients should be advised to seek medical attention if heavy vaginal bleeding persists (soaking a pad or more in less than an hour). Women with heavy, persistent postpartum bleeding should be evaluated for complications such as retained placenta, uterine atony, rarely placenta accreta, or coagulation disorders.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-124054.r6">[6]</xref>&#x000a0;Endometritis may also occur, presenting as fever without an obvious source, and may be accompanied by uterine tenderness and vaginal discharge. This condition usually requires intravenous antibiotics, so&#x000a0;the mother should be advised to seek immediate medical attention.&#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0;</p>
        <p>
<bold>Breastfeeding</bold>
</p>
        <p>Breastfeeding is beneficial for both the mother and the newborn.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-124054.r7">[7]</xref>&#x000a0;Healthcare professionals should encourage women to breastfeed their newborns unless it is contraindicated. Breastfeeding women are less likely to get breast cancer, ovarian cancer, and type 2 diabetes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-124054.r8">[8]</xref>&#x000a0;Breastfeeding reduces the newborn's risk for gastrointestinal tract infections, pediatric cancers, and atopic eczema.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-124054.r8">[8]</xref>&#x000a0;The World Health Organization (WHO) recommends breastfeeding at least&#x000a0;4 to&#x000a0;6 months postpartum, every&#x000a0;3 to&#x000a0;4 hours daily. Breastfeeding should be evaluated at each postnatal visit. Clinicians should assess the mother and infant for any problems with the latch, swallow, nipple type and condition, and positioning during breastfeeding.&#x000a0;Interventions include professional support, peer support, and formal education.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-124054.r9">[9]</xref>&#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0;</p>
        <p>
<bold>Breast engorgement</bold>
</p>
        <p>Women may experience full, firm, and tender breasts after delivery. Frequent breastfeeding on both breasts is recommended to avoid engorgement.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-124054.r10">[10]</xref>&#x000a0;Patients can&#x000a0;use warm washcloths or warm showers or place cold washcloths between feedings to relieve the pain. Women who are not going to breastfeed may be encouraged to use cold packs, wear a well-fitting support bra, and take analgesics as needed.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-124054.r11">[11]</xref>&#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0;</p>
        <p>
<bold>Bladder and bowel function</bold>
</p>
        <p>Voiding must be encouraged and monitored to prevent asymptomatic bladder overfilling. Women are encouraged to use mild laxatives such as docusate, psyllium, and bisacodyl if defecation has not occurred within 3 days of delivery. Another therapeutic consideration is the use of osmotic laxatives, such as polyethylene glycol and lactulose.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-124054.r12">[12]</xref>&#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0;</p>
        <p>
<bold>Nutrition and exercise</bold>
</p>
        <p>Women at higher risk for postpartum weight retention are those with higher gestational weight gain, Black race, and lower socioeconomic status. These factors increase the risk of future obesity and type 2 diabetes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-124054.r13">[13]</xref>&#x000a0;Women should be encouraged to maintain a healthy, balanced diet and gradually return to normal eating habits. Breastfeeding mothers should increase their daily caloric intake by approximately 500 calories to support milk production. During the early postpartum period, strenuous activities should be avoided, with an emphasis on ample rest during the first 2 to 3 weeks. Mothers can gradually ease into low-impact activities, such as walking, and progressively return to their pre-pregnancy routines at a comfortable pace.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-124054.r12">[12]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-124054.r14">[14]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Sexual relations</bold>
</p>
        <p>Libido may decrease after delivery&#x000a0;due to reduced estrogen levels. Prepregnancy libido levels may not return for as long as 1 year postpartum, particularly in women who are breastfeeding. Reassurance is appropriate. Advise women to allow their perineal area to fully heal before resuming sexual activity, typically waiting 4 to 6 weeks to ensure complete recovery. Clinicians must be&#x000a0;comfortable discussing women's sexuality during the early postpartum period and plans for contraception to avoid unintended, closely spaced pregnancy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-124054.r15">[15]</xref>&#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0;</p>
        <p>
<bold>Contraception</bold>
</p>
        <p>The prenatal period is the best time to discuss postpartum contraception.&#x000a0;Adolescents begin motivational interviewing and discussion of long-acting reversible contraception during pregnancy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-124054.r16">[16]</xref>&#x000a0;For breastfeeding women, nonhormonal modalities are usually preferred. ACOG recommends progestin-only contraceptives as the best hormonal contraceptive modality for breastfeeding women. Breastfeeding mothers should not use combination estrogen-progestin contraceptives, as they can interfere with breast milk production.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-124054.r17">[17]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Among hormonal methods, combined estrogen-progestin vaginal rings can be used 4 weeks postpartum. Hormonal methods such as progestin-only oral contraceptives, depot medroxyprogesterone acetate injections, and progestin implants are preferred, as they do not affect milk production.&#x000a0;A vaginal diaphragm and cervical cap should be fitted only after complete involution of the uterus at 6 to 8 weeks postpartum. Intrauterine devices are typically best placed 4 to 6 weeks after delivery.</p>
        <p>Breastfeeding is not an effective contraceptive choice. The lactational amenorrhea method alone or with other forms of contraception has a failure rate of 2% with specific criteria, including that the mother must be breastfeeding exclusively on demand to be amenorrheic, have no vaginal bleeding after 8 weeks postpartum, and have an infant younger than 6 months. This method becomes less reliable as the infant begins eating solid foods.</p>
        <p>Both breastfeeding and nonbreastfeeding women can use barrier contraceptives, intrauterine devices (copper-releasing and hormone-releasing), and progestin-only contraception. WHO recommends that breastfeeding women wait&#x000a0;6 weeks postpartum before starting progestin-only contraceptives. ACOG recommends that combination hormonal contraceptive use should not begin until 3 weeks postpartum because of the increased risk of thromboembolism.&#x000a0;Women should wait at least 6 to 18 months before trying to conceive again.&#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0;</p>
        <p>
<bold>Education</bold>
</p>
        <p>Healthcare professionals should provide essential education on newborn care, including umbilical cord care, bathing, and the importance of immunizations.&#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0;</p>
        <p>
<bold>Miscarriage, stillbirth, or neonatal death</bold>
</p>
        <p>For mothers who experienced pregnancy loss, it is essential to ensure close follow-up. Emotional support, bereavement counseling, and referral are key, including, if appropriate, referrals to counselors and support groups. Also, review of any laboratory or pathology studies related to the loss and counseling regarding recurrent risk and future pregnancy planning are critical.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-124054.r18">[18]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-124054.s4" sec-type="Issues of Concern">
        <title>Issues of Concern</title>
        <p>
<bold>Common Postpartum Concerns</bold>
</p>
        <p>
<bold>Postpartum blues</bold>
</p>
        <p>Transient depression (baby blues) is very common during the first week after delivery.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-124054.r19">[19]</xref>&#x000a0;Women may notice feeling down, anxious, mood swings, crying spells, irritability, and difficulty sleeping. Postpartum blues typically resolve within 2 weeks. Healthcare providers should advise them to seek medical attention if depressive symptoms continue beyond 2 weeks, and if they have difficulty taking care of themselves, the newborn, or have thoughts of harming themselves or the newborn baby.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-124054.r20">[20]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>All women should be screened for mood and anxiety disorders using a validated tool (eg, Edinburgh Postnatal Depression Scale). The American Academy of Pediatrics recommends screening at the 1-, 2-, 4-, and 6-month well visits. Encourage the partner and family members, at least for the first week of the postnatal period, to provide emotional support and care for the newborn. The National Institute for Health and Care Excellence recommends screening all postpartum women for resolution of the postpartum blues 10&#x000a0;to 14 days after delivery.&#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0;</p>
        <p>
<bold>Intimate partner violence</bold>
</p>
        <p>Use HARK (humiliation, afraid, rape, kick) or HITS (hurt, insult, threaten, scream) tools to evaluate for intimate partner violence.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-124054.r21">[21]</xref>&#x000a0;Prioritize patient safety and consider referral to intimate partner violence prevention organizations.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>
<bold>Incontinence</bold>
</p>
        <p>Stress incontinence occurs due to extensive stretching or injury of the pelvic floor muscles during labor. Risk factors for urinary incontinence&#x000a0;3 months postpartum include obesity, parity, smoking, longer duration of breastfeeding, and use of forceps during vaginal delivery. Advise women to do Kegel exercises regularly to strengthen pelvic floor muscles.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-124054.r22">[22]</xref>&#x000a0;Other considerations also include bladder training and weight loss as part of first-line treatment. Letting the new mother know that more than one-fourth of women experience moderate or severe urinary incontinence in the first year postpartum is important.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Hemorrhoids</bold>
</p>
        <p>Hemorrhoids can be caused by constipation or by pushing during the second stage of labor.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-124054.r23">[23]</xref>&#x000a0;The first-line treatment includes increased water and fiber intake, as well as stool softeners. Some may need excision or ligation of refractory hemorrhoids or grade III or higher.&#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0;</p>
        <p>
<bold>Diastisis rectus</bold>
</p>
        <p>This condition refers to the separation of the rectus abdominis muscles along the midline of the abdominal wall. This is a common condition in postpartum women, affecting 30% to 60% of women. Diastasis recti is due to the stretching of the abdominal muscles during pregnancy to accommodate the growing fetus. While diastasis recti is a normal physiological change during pregnancy, it can lead to long-term abdominal weakness, pelvic floor dysfunction, back pain, and issues with body image. A combination of pelvic floor exercises and core strengthening reduced diastasis recti separation and improved function and body image in postpartum women.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-124054.r24">[24]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-124054.s5" sec-type="Clinical Significance">
        <title>Clinical Significance</title>
        <p>According to ACOG, at least 40% of women do not seek postpartum care. Several factors contribute to this trend, such as lack of insurance, lack of adequate family support, low socioeconomic status, poor anticipatory guidance, cultural and racial differences, lack of good transitional care management, and poor access to home visits. According to the Pregnancy Mortality Surveillance System, non-Hispanic Blacks have the highest maternal mortality.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-124054.r25">[25]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-124054.r26">[26]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-124054.r27">[27]</xref></p>
        <p>During the first week of the postnatal period, severe hypertension, severe bleeding, and infection are the most common contributors to maternal deaths, while cardiovascular causes are the leading cause of late deaths.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-124054.r28">[28]</xref>&#x000a0;Compared to developed countries such as Norway and New Zealand, the United States has lagged significantly in providing adequate prenatal care. United States morbidity and mortality are significantly higher despite having the highest healthcare expenditures.&#x000a0;The United States also has significantly fewer maternal healthcare professionals, such as obstetricians and midwives (19 per 1000 live births). Earlier postpartum visits are&#x000a0;crucial&#x000a0;to evaluate the resolution of postpartum blues and chronic medical conditions such as hypertension and diabetes to&#x000a0;improve both maternal and neonatal morbidity and mortality.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-124054.s6" sec-type="Other Issues">
        <title>Other Issues</title>
        <p>
<bold>Health Issues That may Arise During Pregnancy</bold>
</p>
        <p>
<bold>Pregnancy-induced hypertension</bold>
</p>
        <p>Hypertensive disorder risk is higher within 48 hours after delivery. An office visit is recommended within the first 7 days after delivery. Blood pressure of 150/100 mm Hg or higher can be treated with oral medication such as nifedipine or labetalol. Hospitalize if signs of end-organ (liver injury or pulmonary edema) or BP of 160/110 or greater. Lifestyle modification and annual monitoring of BP and body weight are recommended.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Gestational diabetes mellitus</bold>
</p>
        <p>Women with gestational diabetes mellitus (GDM) are at a very high risk of developing diabetes. ACOG recommends that women with GDM have a 75-g, 2-hour fasting oral glucose tolerance test 4 to 12 weeks postpartum to screen for type 2 diabetes mellitus.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-124054.r29">[29]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>
<bold>Thyroid disorders</bold>
</p>
        <p>The mother can experience symptoms of hypo- or hyperthyroidism. The diagnosis of postpartum thyroiditis depends on clinical presentation and elevated free T4 and low thyroid-stimulating hormone. Hyperthyroidism is transient and usually not treated. Beta-blockers can be used if symptoms are present. Hypothyroidism is treated with levothyroxine. The American Thyroid Association recommends annual testing in women with hypothyroidism with a history of postpartum thyroiditis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-124054.r30">[30]</xref>&#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0;</p>
        <p>
<bold>Peripartum cardiomyopathy</bold>
</p>
        <p>This is a form of dilated cardiomyopathy that typically develops in the last month of pregnancy or within the first 5 months postpartum, characterized by left ventricular dysfunction without a preexisting heart condition. The exact cause is not well understood, but there are several linked risk factors, such as advanced maternal age, multiple pregnancies, obesity, hypertension or preeclampsia, multiple gestations, and family history of heart disease. Peripartum cardiomyopathy has symptoms similar to heart failure, and treatment follows the heart failure guidelines. Prognosis varies; some mothers can have a full recovery, while others develop chronic heart failure. For future pregnancies, women who developed Peripartum cardiomyopathy are at risk for recurrence.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-124054.r31">[31]</xref>&#x000a0; &#x000a0;</p>
        <p>
<bold>Deep vein thrombosis/pulmonary embolism</bold>
</p>
        <p>The overall risk of deep vein thrombosis/pulmonary embolism (DVT/PE) is low; the postpartum period represents a time of heightened risk. The overall postpartum incidence of DVT/PE is 1 per 1000 deliveries and accounts for 10% to 15% of deaths. Cesarean delivery, advanced maternal age, preeclampsia, obesity, and thrombophilias increase the risk up to 3 times higher. Those at increased risk should be identified, and appropriate management applied to prevent and, if required, treat.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-124054.r32">[32]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-124054.s7" sec-type="Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes ">
        <title>Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes </title>
        <p>The following are the 2023 WHO recommendations on maternal and newborn care for a positive postnatal experience, which include:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold> Person-centered care:&#x000a0;</bold>Prioritize the physical, emotional, and psychological well-being of women and newborns.&#x000a0;Ensure respectful care, with dignity and privacy, avoiding any form of discrimination.&#x000a0;&#x000a0;Support active participation of women in decision-making regarding their care.&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Skin-to-skin contact: </bold>This promotes immediate, uninterrupted contact between the mother and newborn after birth. Skin-to-skin should be done for at least 1 hour after delivery to promote bonding and breastfeeding.&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Early initiation of breastfeeding: </bold>Encourage the initiation of breastfeeding within the first hour after birth; also encourage exclusive breastfeeding for the first 6 months.&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Postpartum care (for mother and baby):&#x000a0;</bold>Provide a comprehensive postnatal care plan that includes physical, emotional, and social support. Ensure timely visits, ideally within the first 24 to 48 hours, followed by a visit at 7 to 14 days after birth.&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Psychological support:&#x000a0;</bold>Provide mental health screening for maternal depression and anxiety, offering psychological support when needed. Address any feelings of distress or trauma related to the birth process.&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Birth spacing and family planning:&#x000a0;</bold>Counsel women on family planning options and the benefits of birth spacing to ensure long-term health and well-being. Discuss contraceptive options soon after delivery, considering the woman&#x02019;s personal and cultural preferences.&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Addressing the needs of vulnerable populations:&#x000a0;</bold>Provide tailored care for women and newborns from marginalized, vulnerable, or disadvantaged groups. Ensure accessibility and inclusivity of care services, including for women with disabilities, adolescents, or those living in remote areas.&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Continuity of care:&#x000a0;</bold>Ensure seamless coordination among birth attendants, community health workers, and other healthcare professionals to maintain continuity of care. Offer services that integrate maternal, newborn, and child health, including mental health support, into routine postnatal care.&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Support for partners and families:&#x000a0;</bold>Involve partners and families in the postnatal care process to provide emotional, social, and practical support.&#x000a0;Encourage positive family engagement, particularly in promoting maternal well-being.&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Health system strengthening:&#x000a0;</bold>Ensure healthcare systems are equipped to deliver high-quality, accessible, and timely postnatal care. Train and support health workers to deliver compassionate, evidence-based care.&#x000a0; These recommendations aim to create a supportive environment that promotes physical and mental well-being for both mothers and newborns during the critical postnatal period.[2023 WHO&#x000a0;<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789240045989">https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789240045989</ext-link>]</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-124054.s8" sec-type="Nursing, Allied Health, and Interprofessional Team Interventions">
        <title>Nursing, Allied Health, and Interprofessional Team Interventions</title>
        <p>Ultimately, providing the optimum healthcare and support for postpartum families requires local, state-wide, and national-level policy changes. Even though the Affordable Care Act improved access to maternal care, the United States still needs a major policy change to provide universal, appropriate, evidence-based, and culturally competent access to maternity care.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-124054.r33">[33]</xref>&#x000a0;Expanding Medicaid eligibility, which covers almost half of the deliveries in the United States, can improve postpartum coverage.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-124054.r34">[34]</xref> This should be facilitated through mutual support between healthcare providers and insurance platforms by appropriate reimbursement levels that support&#x02014;and indeed foster&#x02014;postpartum care as a continuous, rather than an isolated, process, which undoubtedly leads to positive outcomes for the community.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-124054.r35">[35]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-124054.s9">
        <title>Review Questions</title>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=124054&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=124054">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
